module gunbc.rung_drop.browser_ready_labels_fleet_route_stall

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.rung_drop { RungDrop, Standing, AuthoredProse, BrowserReadyLabelsFleetRouteStall }

// THE READINESS-LABEL CONVERGE'S PRODUCTION FLEET ROUTE EXECUTES NOWHERE, AND THIS ROW IS WHERE
// THAT IS TRACKED (review round on gunbc#13401; the manager's ruling: the stall is rostered here,
// not left as a module-local condition row). The converge's transport is always FleetSsh
// (browser_toolchain_admin_transport), and BOTH shared host-effect runners -- the argv runner and
// the argv+stdin runner -- return RunArgvTransportRefused for FleetSsh before any leg runs; the
// arm's own fail-closed note says admitting that capability "is its own change with its own
// analysis". So grant, revoke and daemon-reload never execute over fleet, on CI or in a bootstrap,
// and the route's outcome is always BrowserReadyLabelsFailed there. The steps DO execute for real
// in the changed-witness receipts (test.claim.runner.browser_ready_label_real_path_witness): the
// production converge over LocalShell, the unprivileged posture, against a mktemp scratch
// destination.
data browser_ready_labels_fleet_route_stall: RungDrop = RungDrop {
  identity: "browser_ready_labels_fleet_route_stall" as NonEmptyStr,

  subject: "Real execution of the runner readiness-label converge over its production fleet route",

  declared: "2026-10-06",

  standing: Standing,

  declaration: AuthoredProse { legacy: BrowserReadyLabelsFleetRouteStall, authored: "The readiness-label converge over fleet -- **RUNG STALL, DECLARED (2026-10-06).** PREVIOUS RUNG: none to lower -- the production route never executed: the deleted shell path was also carried by a transport that refused before any leg, and the typed argv steps are carried by the same posture, so this change newly breaks nothing; it declares what the route already was. THE STALL: in a real bootstrap the converge's transport is always FleetSsh (gunbc.runner_browser_toolchain browser_toolchain_admin_transport), and both shared host-effect runners (run_argv_over_host_effect_transport, run_argv_stdin_over_host_effect_transport) return RunArgvTransportRefused for FleetSsh before any leg runs -- the arm's own note: admitting the capability is its own change with its own analysis, fail-closed until that change names itself. Grant, revoke and daemon-reload therefore never run over fleet, and the route's outcome is always BrowserReadyLabelsFailed. TEMPORARY RUNG: the step pairing is proven by real execution elsewhere -- the LocalRepoWet receipts (test.claim.runner.browser_ready_label_real_path_witness) drive the production converge itself over LocalShell with the unprivileged posture against a mktemp scratch destination (grant writes and is read back, revoke removes and the read-back fails, the unprivileged reload is a typed posture skip), and the hermetic witness asserts the typed plans' steps every run. REASON: the capability the route needs -- FleetSsh argv execution and argv+stdin execution through exec_as_fleet_principal -- belongs to the shared transport's own analysis (the fleet_subsumption arc), and a per-consumer bypass was ruled out as a second dispatch beside the shared runners. POPULATION: bounded at one route -- the readiness-label converge's production transport. RESTORATION TRIGGER: the shared host-effect transport admits the FleetSsh arm -- argv execution and argv+stdin execution over exec_as_fleet_principal -- by the fleet_subsumption arc's own change, with no per-consumer bypass; a safe reload realization or an isolated manager is the companion question that admission names. The receipts' executions over LocalShell are unaffected by, and do not discharge, this trigger." }
}
